# Check answers before saving

Show a summary before a multi-step setup changes anything, and make the title and final button say what will happen. These examples are local guidance. They do not save a scene or call a device.

## When to use it

Use it after a multi-step scene, schedule, notification or dashboard setup. A single reversible change, such as brightness, does not need a review page. For an irreversible action, use [Pause before an exceptional consequence](interruption.html) as well. A long list of answers is not a warning.

<%= example('nhs-check-answers-scene') %>

## Say what saving does

Separate saving a scene from running it, saving an automation from enabling it, and changing a dashboard from changing a device. In the example, “Saving adds the scene to your controls. It will not change any lights now.” List the devices, area, units, time zone and activation state where they affect the result. Show a skipped optional answer as “Not provided”. Do not show questions from branches the person did not take.

Give each Change link an accessible name that identifies the answer, such as “Change room”. After the edit, return to the summary with the other answers kept. If the edit needs a new question, ask it first, then return. Do not restart the whole wizard for one change.

<%= example('nhs-check-answers-changed') %>

## Handle changes during review

An entity can become unavailable while a person reviews a draft. Check permissions and capabilities again before saving. Show which answer needs attention and keep the rest. Do not swap in another lamp, change a temperature unit or enable a newly found device without asking.

If another household member has edited the same configuration, show the conflict and let the person reload or reconcile the draft. Do not show a success message for a rejected update.

## Build it accessibly

- Use a description list with readable labels, in the same order as the questions.
- Let each row stack at narrow widths instead of squeezing the action column.
- On a failed save, focus the error summary and link each error to its answer.
- Do not rely on icons or colour to explain a row.

See [Summary list](../components/summary-list.html) for the markup and the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html) for testing.

## Keep the boundary clear

Production code must treat validation, saving, activation and observed device state as four separate facts. Block a second submit while the save is pending, announce the outcome and keep a safe way to retry if the result is unknown. Keep Home Assistant's own permission checks and service behaviour. Show the result with [Confirm completed household tasks](confirmation.html).
